[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"C# Attributes: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Attributes in C# provide a powerful way to add declarative information to code elements such as classes, methods, properties, and fields. They act as metadata, offering instructions to the compiler, runtime environment, or other tools about how the code should be treated. This tutorial will guide you through the fundamentals of attributes, their usage, and custom attribute creation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Attributes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Attributes are classes that inherit from the 'System.Attribute' base class (directly or indirectly). They are applied to code elements using square brackets '[]' followed by the attribute's name. Attributes can have parameters, which provide additional information."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Built-in Attributes"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# provides several built-in attributes for common tasks. Here are a few examples:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'Obsolete': Marks a code element as deprecated, optionally providing a message and indicating whether its use should result in a compiler error."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'Conditional': Specifies that the execution of a method depends on a specified preprocessor symbol."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'DebuggerDisplay': Controls how an object is displayed in the debugger."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// Example using the Obsolete attribute
[Obsolete("This method is deprecated. Use NewMethod instead.", true)]
public void OldMethod() {
  // ...
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, 'OldMethod' is marked as obsolete. The first parameter is the message displayed to the developer, and the second parameter (true) indicates that using 'OldMethod' will result in a compiler error."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Custom Attributes"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can create your own attributes to add custom metadata to your code. To do this, you need to:"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a class that inherits from 'System.Attribute'."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Define the attribute's properties (if any)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the 'AttributeUsage' attribute to specify where the custom attribute can be applied (e.g., classes, methods, properties)."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Define a custom attribute
[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class | AttributeTargets.Method, AllowMultiple = false)]
public class AuthorAttribute : System.Attribute
{
  public string Name { get; set; }
  public string Version { get; set; }

  public AuthorAttribute(string name)
  {
    Name = name;
  }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'AuthorAttribute' can be applied to classes and methods. The 'AllowMultiple' property of 'AttributeUsage' is set to 'false', meaning that only one 'AuthorAttribute' can be applied to a single element."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Custom Attributes"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"// Apply the custom attribute
[Author("John Doe", Version = "1.0")]
public class MyClass
{
  [Author("Jane Smith")]
  public void MyMethod() {
    // ...
  }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, the 'AuthorAttribute' is applied to both the 'MyClass' class and the 'MyMethod' method. Notice that the 'Name' parameter is passed through the constructor, while the 'Version' parameter is set using a property initializer."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Retrieving Attribute Information at Runtime"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can retrieve attribute information at runtime using reflection. The 'System.Reflection' namespace provides classes and methods for inspecting metadata."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Reflection;

// Retrieving attribute information
Type type = typeof(MyClass);
object[] attributes = type.GetCustomAttributes(typeof(AuthorAttribute), true);

foreach (AuthorAttribute attribute in attributes)
{
  Console.WriteLine("Author: " + attribute.Name);
  Console.WriteLine("Version: " + attribute.Version);
}"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code retrieves all 'AuthorAttribute' instances applied to the 'MyClass' type and prints the 'Name' and 'Version' properties to the console."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"AttributeUsage Attribute"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'AttributeUsage' attribute is crucial when defining custom attributes. It controls:"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The code elements to which the attribute can be applied (e.g., classes, methods, properties, fields, events, interfaces, structs)."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Whether the attribute can be applied multiple times to the same element ('AllowMultiple')."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Whether the attribute is inherited by derived classes ('Inherited')."},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class | AttributeTargets.Interface, AllowMultiple = true, Inherited = false)]
public class MyAttribute : System.Attribute
{
  // ...
}"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'MyAttribute' can be applied to classes and interfaces, can be applied multiple times to the same element, and is not inherited by derived classes."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Attributes are a powerful feature in C# that allows you to add metadata to your code. They can be used to provide instructions to the compiler, runtime environment, or other tools. By understanding how to create and use custom attributes, you can enhance the flexibility and maintainability of your C# applications."}]
